From: skyjet18 <57456827+skyjet18@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 11:16:59 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 50/50] hls: added support for #EXT-X-DISCONTINUITY

Ported to FFmpeg 9.0.2 from skyjet18/FFmpeg release/6.1-patched 350749ce97
(subject typo "hlc" fixed).

This changes HLS for every libavformat user: at a discontinuity the
segment demuxer is restarted and the timestamps of the new region are
shifted so that the stream stays continuous (an ad stitched into a
programme no longer makes the timestamps jump), and a playlist that is
switched in during playback is positioned by timestamp instead of by
media sequence number.

Without a #EXT-X-DISCONTINUITY tag the demuxer behaves as without this
patch.

Port: read_data_continuous() of 9.0.2 is structured differently
(reload_playlist(), input_read_done cleared on entry, byte-range
connection reuse with input_reuse/segment_reusable()). The region check
sits where a segment is opened and after the advance to the next
segment. discontinuity_restart() closes input and input_next: with
http_persistent (the default) the kept connection would otherwise be read
on as the new segment, since the top of the function clears
input_read_done, and the first segment of the region would be skipped.
Neither segment_reusable() nor the byte-range extension of open_input()
joins a range across a discontinuity. 0046: EVENT playlists already
select by timestamp, the new live branch sits behind it. 0048: after the
resync to the live edge the next segment open compares the region and
restarts the demuxer if it changed.

Gating: a playlist gets has_discontinuity when a parse sees the tag, kept
across reloads. Demuxer restarts, timestamp offsets, selection by
timestamp, the renumbering of regions and the first_timestamp rule below
only apply to such playlists; for the others the region number is only
tracked, so that a tag showing up later compares against the right one.
The start alignment of hls_read_header() ("one segment behind, sync it
up") is kept as it was, the region is taken from the segment actually
started on.

Review: the original continued a region at first_timestamp plus the
durations from the first segment of the playlist, which is wrong for a
live window. Each playlist anchors its timeline on the segment of the
first packet after a start, seek or switch in (timeline_seq_no,
timeline_time); a reload moves the anchor forward only when its segment
dropped out of the window. A media sequence that went back (lagging CDN
edge) keeps the anchor and the region numbers aligned on a segment both
lists contain, by media sequence or by URL; a reload that shares no
segment with the previous list drops the anchor (the time in between is
not estimated from the target duration; segment_start_time() then counts
from first_timestamp) and, without #EXT-X-DISCONTINUITY-SEQUENCE, starts
a new local region. A sequence restart (the 0048 case) is a new region
only in playlists that had discontinuities, a stream without the tag keeps
its raw timestamps. With discontinuities first_timestamp of a live
playlist counts back to the window start, from the first segment of the
region. select_cur_seq_no() keeps c->cur_seq_no if it is in the window
and the playlist shares the numbering (same region at that number as a
playlist being read, when both carry #EXT-X-DISCONTINUITY-SEQUENCE), and
selects by timestamp otherwise, but only from an anchor that has followed
the playlist (else c->cur_seq_no if in the window).
The offsets of the regions are kept in HLSContext (the first one stored
for a region stays): shared between playlists only for regions numbered
by #EXT-X-DISCONTINUITY-SEQUENCE, per playlist otherwise, so that local
numbers never match another playlist; a seek back into a known region or
a playlist switched in later reuses the offset instead of estimating it
again. Subtitle playlists check the region when a segment is opened
(after the reload), take the offset of the audio/video playlists when
there is one and estimate it from the first cue otherwise, without
storing it. hls_read_seek() and recheck_discard_flags() take the region
of the segment they land on and drop a pending restart; current_segment()
returns NULL for a sequence number before the window instead of indexing
before the array. A misplaced or invalid #EXT-X-DISCONTINUITY-SEQUENCE is
ignored with a warning instead of rejecting the playlist.
Known limitation (as in the original): the offset of a region is taken
from its first packet, audio or video, whose dts may differ by a few
frames; inside a region all streams share the offset, only the boundary
between regions can show that difference as a small gap or overlap.

FATE tests fate-hls-discontinuity and fate-hls-discontinuity-seek as in
the original, in the FATE_HLSENC_LAVFI list of 9.0.2 (the FATE_HLSENC list
requires SAMPLES there, the tests need none); the reference files are
regenerated with 9.0.2, whose framecrc no longer prints the mpegts stream
id side data ("S=1, 1"), the timestamps are those of the original.
fate-hls-discontinuity-notag-live reads a live playlist without the tag;
its reference is the output of the demuxer without this patch.
